   "created_utc": "2014-04-24T14:20:42Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "Have a peek at the version of new which takes a <code>::std::nothrow&amp;</code> as its second parameter"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 23271687,
   "parent_id": 23271495,
   "author_user_id": 166749,
   "author_display_name": "Fred Foo",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/23271687",
   "score": 30,
   "created_utc": "2014-04-24T14:22:43Z",
   "last_edit_utc": "2020-01-03T10:24:44Z",
   "last_activity_utc": "2020-01-03T10:24:44Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
   "body_html": "<p>The main differences, aside from syntax and <code>free</code> vs. <code>delete</code>, are</p>\n\n<ol>\n<li>you can portably <a href=\"http://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/memory/new/operator_new#Global_replacements\" rel=\"noreferrer\">replace</a> <code>::operator new</code>;</li>\n<li><code>malloc</code> comes with <code>realloc</code>, for which <code>new</code> has no equivalent;</li>\n<li><code>new</code> has the concept of a <a href=\"http://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/memory/new/new_handler\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><code>new_handler</code></a>, for which there is no <code>malloc</code> equivalent.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>(Replacing <code>malloc</code> opens up a <a href=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20170204072041/https://www.iecc.com/linker/linker09.html\" rel=\"noreferrer\">can of worms</a>. It can be done, but not portably, because it requires knowledge of the linker.)</p>\n"
  },
